WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.878
Diffusion is net movement from
higher to lower concentration.

00:00:03.657 --> 00:00:08.004
And the carbon dioxide enters through
the stomata, little leaf doors?

00:00:08.691 --> 00:00:09.198
Pause.

00:00:09.243 --> 00:00:09.727
Yes.

00:00:10.029 --> 00:00:12.823
Guard cells change shape
to open or close them.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.422
Closing reduces water loss but also
restricts carbon dioxide entry.

00:00:17.771 --> 00:00:20.391
The waxy cuticle reduces water loss too.

00:00:21.096 --> 00:00:24.050
Then where does the water delivery arrive?

00:00:24.842 --> 00:00:27.362
Root hair cells absorb
water from the soil.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.949
Xylem vessels carry it up to leaves.

00:00:30.351 --> 00:00:33.669
Evaporation of water from
leaves, called transpiration,

00:00:33.896 --> 00:00:35.642
helps pull more water upward.

00:00:36.021 --> 00:00:39.894
Phloem carries dissolved sugars,
mainly sucrose, from where they

00:00:39.897 --> 00:00:41.526
are made to where they are needed.

00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:43.741
Xylem brings water.

00:00:44.186 --> 00:00:45.794
Phloem moves food sugars.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:47.427
Different delivery vans.

00:00:48.231 --> 00:00:48.628
Good.

00:00:49.038 --> 00:00:51.260
Oxygen can diffuse out through stomata.

00:00:51.635 --> 00:00:55.275
The direction of net gas exchange
depends on which processes are faster.

00:00:55.468 --> 00:00:56.420
We'll return to that.

00:00:56.766 --> 00:01:00.929
Imagine a tree gains lots of dry mass
while the soil loses very little.

00:01:01.355 --> 00:01:03.467
Where did much of the
tree's carbon come from?

00:01:04.291 --> 00:01:06.073
From carbon dioxide in the air.

00:01:06.621 --> 00:01:09.289
So a wooden table is partly captured air?

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:12.007
Its carbon came through that route.

00:01:12.422 --> 00:01:17.347
Photosynthesis builds glucose, and plants
turn glucose into other substances.

00:01:17.883 --> 00:01:21.663
Water and minerals matter too,
but the soil is not supplying a

00:01:21.666 --> 00:01:23.927
tree-sized pile of ready-made food.

00:01:24.643 --> 00:01:26.704
What jobs does the glucose have?

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:31.441
First, respiration releases
energy from it for life processes.

00:01:31.852 --> 00:01:35.856
Second, spare glucose can be
converted into starch for storage.

00:01:36.332 --> 00:01:39.875
Third, it can help make
cellulose for strong cell walls.

00:01:40.195 --> 00:01:44.205
Fourth, it supplies building material
for oils and other substances.

00:01:44.953 --> 00:01:48.879
Why store starch instead of keeping
a giant sugary swimming pool?

00:01:49.699 --> 00:01:51.072
Glucose is soluble.

00:01:51.638 --> 00:01:54.902
Lots of dissolved glucose would
affect water movement into the cell.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:59.183
Starch is insoluble, so it is a
useful compact store with much

00:01:59.184 --> 00:02:00.727
less effect on water balance.

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:02.408
And protein?

00:02:02.871 --> 00:02:05.287
Does glucose magically become nitrogen?

00:02:06.053 --> 00:02:06.474
No.

00:02:07.092 --> 00:02:08.726
Remember our atom accountant.

00:02:09.304 --> 00:02:12.766
Plants need nitrate ions from
the soil to supply nitrogen

00:02:12.777 --> 00:02:14.643
for amino acids and proteins.

00:02:15.153 --> 00:02:17.297
A nitrate shortage can stunt growth.

00:02:17.626 --> 00:02:20.086
Magnesium ions are needed
to make chlorophyll.

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:23.752
A shortage can cause yellow
leaves and reduce photosynthesis.

00:02:24.547 --> 00:02:28.325
So mineral fertilizer provides
ingredients, not a replacement

00:02:28.327 --> 00:02:30.015
for light and photosynthesis.

00:02:30.825 --> 00:02:31.634
Exactly.

00:02:32.102 --> 00:02:35.685
A seedling can grow briefly
in darkness using stored food.

00:02:36.068 --> 00:02:39.491
Once reserves run low, it
needs enough photosynthesis to

00:02:39.491 --> 00:02:41.297
replace what respiration uses.

00:02:41.733 --> 00:02:45.809
Watering a plant in a dark cupboard
does not solve the missing energy input.

00:02:46.563 --> 00:02:50.888
Note to self, a watering
can is not a substitute sun

00:02:51.288 --> 00:02:54.366
Our greenhouse grower buys a
brighter lamp, but the plant's

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:56.369
photosynthesis rate does not rise.

00:02:56.835 --> 00:02:58.249
Has the plant become lazy?

00:02:59.061 --> 00:03:02.241
Obviously, it needs a motivational poster.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:05.222
It needs the limiting factor addressed.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.878
A limiting factor is a
condition restricting the rate.

00:03:09.262 --> 00:03:11.347
More light helps when light is limiting.

00:03:11.788 --> 00:03:15.375
Once carbon dioxide or temperature
limits the process, extra

00:03:15.378 --> 00:03:16.859
light alone may do little.

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:19.911
The graph rises, then levels off.

00:03:20.401 --> 00:03:22.659
The plateau is not the plant being full.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:23.955
Correct.

00:03:24.322 --> 00:03:28.476
If we increase carbon dioxide under
suitable conditions, rate can rise

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.195
until another factor limits it.

00:03:30.595 --> 00:03:31.678
Temperature is different.

00:03:31.887 --> 00:03:34.701
Warming a cold plant can
speed enzyme-controlled

00:03:34.702 --> 00:03:36.315
reactions up to an optimum.

00:03:37.123 --> 00:03:41.125
Then I turn the greenhouse
into an oven for maximum salad?

00:03:41.859 --> 00:03:42.868
Absolutely not.

00:03:43.277 --> 00:03:47.672
Beyond a suitable temperature, enzymes
and other processes are damaged, water

00:03:47.672 --> 00:03:49.663
stress can increase, and rate falls.

00:03:50.024 --> 00:03:53.003
There is no one exact best
temperature for every plant.

00:03:53.356 --> 00:03:55.994
The curve rises to an optimum, then falls.

00:03:56.807 --> 00:04:00.747
Light, carbon dioxide,
temperature: find the bottleneck

00:04:00.771 --> 00:04:02.258
before buying more equipment.

00:04:03.003 --> 00:04:04.886
Let's measure a rate with pondweed.

00:04:05.295 --> 00:04:08.343
Under suitable light, bubbles
of oxygen may leave it.

00:04:08.763 --> 00:04:12.854
Count bubbles per minute as a rough
measure or collect gas volume over a

00:04:12.854 --> 00:04:14.844
fixed time for a better comparison.

00:04:15.671 --> 00:04:18.670
Are 10 bubbles always
twice as much gas as five?

00:04:19.508 --> 00:04:20.946
Not if their sizes differ.

00:04:21.345 --> 00:04:24.919
Some gas also dissolves, so
bubble counting is approximate.

00:04:25.253 --> 00:04:27.140
Change one factor at a time.

00:04:27.541 --> 00:04:31.714
For a light test, vary lamp distance
while keeping plant length, carbon

00:04:31.714 --> 00:04:35.297
dioxide supply, temperature,
and counting time controlled.

00:04:36.118 --> 00:04:40.000
The lamp could warm the water, so
I'd accidentally test two factors.
